A product is selected at random and found to be defective. What is the probability that it was produced by Factory Y?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$\\frac{4}{25}$"},{"key":"B","text":"$\\frac{9}{25}$"},{"key":"C","text":"$\\frac{16}{25}$"},{"key":"D","text":"$\\frac{9}{50}$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'This is a conditional probability problem. Given that the product is defective, the sample space is the $50$ defective products. Of those, $32$ came from Factory Y. The conditional probability is $\\frac{32}{50} = \\frac{16}{25}$. The value $\\frac{4}{25}$ comes from dividing $32$ by the total of $200$ Factory Y products, computing $P(\\text{defective} \\mid \\text{Factory Y})$ rather than the requested $P(\\text{Factory Y} \\mid \\text{defective})$. The value $\\frac{9}{25}$ comes from using Factory X''s defective count $18$ in the numerator instead of Factory Y''s count. The value $\\frac{9}{50}$ results from dividing $18$ by $100$ due to misidentifying both the numerator and denominator.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"equation_setup_error"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-16"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-16","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);







$$\\begin{cases} 4x - 6y = 10 \\\\ -2x + 3y = k \\end{cases}$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, '-5', ARRAY['-5'], E'A system of linear equations has infinitely many solutions when the two equations represent the same line. Multiply the second equation by $-2$: $-2(-2x + 3y) = -2k$, which gives $4x - 6y = -2k$. For this to be identical to the first equation $4x - 6y = 10$, the right-hand sides must be equal: $-2k = 10$, so $k = -5$. A common error is forgetting the negative sign when multiplying by $-2$, which gives $k = 5$. Another error is dividing $10$ by $2$ without accounting for the sign, yielding $k = 5$. Setting $k = 0$ reflects a misconception that the constant must be zero for dependent systems.', NULL, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-16"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-16","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);















$$\\begin{cases} 2x + y = 11 \\\\ x = 3 \\end{cases}$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$3$"},{"key":"B","text":"$5$"},{"key":"C","text":"$8$"},{"key":"D","text":"$17$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'Substitute $x = 3$ into the first equation: $2(3) + y = 11$, which gives $6 + y = 11$. Subtract $6$ from both sides to get $y = 5$. The value $3$ is the given value of $x$, not the requested value of $y$. The value $8$ results from computing $11 - 3$ instead of $11 - 2(3)$, forgetting to multiply $3$ by $2$. The value $17$ comes from adding $2(3)$ to $11$ instead of subtracting.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"misread_question"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"arithmetic_slip"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"equation_setup_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 45, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-16"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-16","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);











What is the value of $x$ in the equation above?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, '1', ARRAY['1'], E'Since $x^2 - 9 = (x - 3)(x + 3)$, multiply both sides by $(x - 3)(x + 3)$ to clear the denominators: $5(x + 3) - 2(x - 3) = 24$. Expanding gives $5x + 15 - 2x + 6 = 24$, which simplifies to $3x + 21 = 24$. Subtracting $21$ from both sides yields $3x = 3$, so $x = 1$. Since $x = 1$ does not make any denominator zero, it is a valid solution.', NULL, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-16"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-16","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);





Education researcher Yuki Tanaka contends that standardized test scores primarily reflect a student''s access to preparation resources — tutoring, practice materials, and test-taking strategies — rather than innate academic ability. She argues that the strong correlation between family income and test performance supports this interpretation, suggesting that the tests measure economic advantage as much as intellectual readiness.

Text 2
Education researcher Marcus Obi acknowledges that socioeconomic factors influence standardized test scores but maintains that the tests still provide meaningful information about student achievement. He notes that when researchers control for income and school quality, test scores continue to predict college performance, indicating that the assessments capture genuine academic skills alongside the effects of economic background.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Tanaka views test scores as primarily reflecting economic advantage, while Obi views them as capturing real academic skills despite socioeconomic influences."},{"key":"B","text":"Tanaka believes standardized tests should be eliminated entirely, while Obi believes they should be reformed to reduce bias."},{"key":"C","text":"Tanaka focuses on how tests are scored, while Obi focuses on how tests are designed."},{"key":"D","text":"Tanaka argues that test scores do not correlate with any measurable outcomes, while Obi argues they correlate only with family income."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The correct answer is the choice stating that Tanaka views scores as reflecting economic advantage while Obi views them as capturing real academic skills despite socioeconomic influences. Text 1 explicitly argues that tests "measure economic advantage as much as intellectual readiness," and Text 2 counters that scores "capture genuine academic skills alongside the effects of economic background." The choice about eliminating versus reforming tests goes beyond what either text states; neither researcher proposes a specific policy action regarding test use. The choice about scoring versus design misidentifies both researchers'' focuses; Tanaka discusses what scores reflect, and Obi discusses what scores predict, but neither examines scoring procedures or test construction. The choice about correlations contradicts both texts: Tanaka does identify a correlation between income and performance, and Obi identifies a correlation between scores and college performance even after controlling for income.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-16"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-16","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



Neuroscientist Laura Simmons argues that sleep is essential for memory consolidation. Her studies show that participants who slept for eight hours after learning a list of words recalled significantly more items the following day than those who remained awake for the same period.

Text 2
Neuroscientist Paul Okoro builds on research like Simmons''s but emphasizes that not all sleep is equally beneficial for memory. His experiments indicate that slow-wave sleep, which occurs primarily during the first half of the night, is particularly important for consolidating factual knowledge, whereas REM sleep supports the retention of procedural skills such as playing a musical instrument.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By disputing the claim that sleep has any measurable effect on word recall"},{"key":"B","text":"By suggesting that Simmons's participants did not sleep long enough to experience memory benefits"},{"key":"C","text":"By accepting that sleep aids memory but specifying which stages of sleep contribute most to different types of memory"},{"key":"D","text":"By acknowledging that sleep benefits memory but arguing that only factual knowledge, not procedural skills, is consolidated during sleep"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'The correct answer is the choice stating that Okoro would accept that sleep aids memory but specify which stages contribute to different memory types. Text 2 explicitly states that Okoro "builds on" research like Simmons''s, indicating agreement with the general finding, while adding the distinction between slow-wave sleep for factual knowledge and REM sleep for procedural skills. The choice about disputing sleep''s effect on recall contradicts Text 2, which accepts the link between sleep and memory. The choice about insufficient sleep duration introduces a criticism that appears nowhere in Text 2; Okoro''s focus is on sleep stages, not sleep length. The choice about only factual knowledge being consolidated misrepresents Okoro''s position, which identifies two types of memory consolidated during different sleep stages, not a restriction to one type.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-16"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-16","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);









- Fermentation has been used for thousands of years to preserve food and produce beverages such as wine and beer.
- In the nineteenth century, Louis Pasteur demonstrated that fermentation is caused by living microorganisms, not by spontaneous chemical reactions.
- Modern biotechnology companies use engineered fermentation processes to produce pharmaceuticals, including insulin and certain vaccines.
- Traditional fermentation relies on naturally occurring microorganisms, while industrial fermentation often uses genetically modified organisms.
- Some consumer advocacy groups have raised concerns about the safety and labeling of products made through engineered fermentation.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Louis Pasteur's discovery that fermentation is driven by living microorganisms laid the groundwork for modern biotechnology companies that now use engineered fermentation to produce pharmaceuticals such as insulin."},{"key":"B","text":"Although fermentation has been used for thousands of years to preserve food and produce beverages, modern biotechnology companies use engineered fermentation processes to manufacture pharmaceuticals — a development that has prompted consumer advocacy groups to raise concerns about the safety and labeling of such products."},{"key":"C","text":"Traditional fermentation relies on naturally occurring microorganisms to produce foods and beverages, while industrial fermentation uses genetically modified organisms to create products such as insulin and vaccines."},{"key":"D","text":"Consumer advocacy groups have raised concerns about the safety and labeling of products made through engineered fermentation, a process that biotechnology companies use to produce pharmaceuticals including insulin and certain vaccines."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The choice beginning with "Although fermentation has been used for thousands of years" accomplishes all three parts of the rhetorical goal: it establishes the traditional use (food preservation and beverages), introduces the modern use (engineered fermentation for pharmaceuticals), and acknowledges the concern raised by consumer advocacy groups about safety and labeling. The choice about Pasteur''s discovery traces a historical connection between early microbiology and modern biotechnology but omits any mention of consumer concerns. The choice contrasting naturally occurring and genetically modified organisms effectively presents the traditional-versus-modern distinction but does not address any concerns about the modern approach. The choice foregrounding consumer advocacy concerns leads with the worry rather than presenting a balanced contrast, and it omits any mention of the traditional uses of fermentation.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 120, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-16"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-16","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



- The axolotl is a salamander native to Lake Xochimilco in Mexico City.
- Unlike most amphibians, axolotls retain their larval features throughout adulthood, a phenomenon called neoteny.
- Axolotls can regenerate entire limbs, portions of their spinal cord, heart tissue, and parts of their brain.
- Scientists study axolotl regeneration to gain insights that might advance human wound healing and organ repair.
- Wild axolotl populations have declined sharply due to habitat loss and water pollution in Lake Xochimilco.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"The axolotl, a salamander native to Lake Xochimilco in Mexico City, has experienced sharp population declines due to habitat loss and water pollution."},{"key":"B","text":"Because axolotls can regenerate entire limbs, spinal cord tissue, and even parts of their brain, scientists study them to develop insights that may advance human wound healing and organ repair."},{"key":"C","text":"The axolotl is notable for retaining its larval features throughout adulthood, a phenomenon known as neoteny that distinguishes it from most other amphibians."},{"key":"D","text":"Native to Lake Xochimilco in Mexico City, the axolotl is an unusual salamander that retains larval features and has declined sharply in the wild due to habitat loss."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The choice connecting the axolotl''s regenerative abilities to scientific research on human wound healing and organ repair directly emphasizes the scientific significance of these abilities, fulfilling the stated rhetorical goal. The choice about population declines focuses on conservation concerns rather than the axolotl''s biological significance to science. The choice about neoteny describes an interesting biological trait but does not connect it to any scientific application or broader significance. The choice combining habitat information with neoteny addresses the animal''s ecology and one trait without establishing why either matters to scientific research.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-16"}$